WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.040
Have you ever wondered
how talking therapy works?

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:04.640
Maybe you've suffered a bereavement,

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.920
been feeling down or anxious
or just overwhelmed

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.520
and you're starting to think
you should talk to someone.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.800
Perhaps you've tried talking
to friends or family,

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:15.080
but they don't seem
to quite understand

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.360
or they have too much
on their plates.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.920
So you consider therapy,
but you have doubts.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:23.760
What if you're not ill enough
to see a professional?

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.680
Will it feel awkward
talking to a stranger?

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.160
Will they make you lie down
on a couch and reveal

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:30.840
your innermost secrets?

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:38.560
In the UK, you can get
talking therapy free on the NHS,

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.520
but waiting times
can vary significantly.

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:45.120
Private therapists can be expensive,
although some do offer lower fees

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.320
for those who can't afford
to pay more.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.480
But let's say you've arranged
your first session

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.600
and you're wondering
how exactly it might help.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:55.120
The good news is
that therapy can actually change

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:58.200
the way your brain functions
in a positive way.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:02.080
Our brains are a little bit
like Play-Doh, capable of adapting,

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.400
reshaping and rebuilding.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:07.040
This is known as neural plasticity.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.120
Whilst in therapy, your brain
circuits are able to remould

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.160
and reconnect in different ways,

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:16.240
meaning we're able to reframe
negative or obsessive thinking.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:20.320
By shifting our thinking, we start
to strengthen different pathways.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:23.480
So when you talk through stressful
feelings and strong emotions

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:27.080
in therapy, you're rewiring
your brain, a bit like rewiring

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.760
an electrical circuit.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.440
Therapy also activates
the hormone serotonin

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:35.160
and can help improve our ability
to cope with stress.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.280
Studies found the brains in people
who've had therapy for depression

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:41.360
and post-traumatic stress disorder
showed improvements

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:45.640
that were as good
as SSRI antidepressant treatments.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:48.760
Even short courses of therapy
can be beneficial.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.840
They often use techniques
known as CBT,

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:54.400
or cognitive behavioural therapy.

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:57.200
This can be particularly useful
in helping you change

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:00.200
specific behaviours
or negative thinking patterns

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:03.520
because it focuses attention
on your problems

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:06.400
and helps you develop
coping strategies.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:11.360
In 2020-21, over 630,000 people

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.520
completed the NHS Talking Therapy
programme in England,

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.920
averaging seven and a half sessions
with a therapist.

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:21.640
51.4% of those who completed
the programme for anxiety

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:23.680
or depression recovered.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:26.800
The most important factor
determining whether therapy works

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:29.640
for you is the quality
of the relationship you have

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:30.960
with your therapist.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:34.360
This is actually more important
than the type of therapy you choose

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.640
or the therapist's experience.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:39.600
So if you don't get on
with the first therapist you meet,

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:42.880
it's worth persevering
and looking for a new one.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:45.600
Your level of motivation
is also important,

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:47.560
as is the therapist's ability

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.400
to recognise
which type of support is best.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:53.080
Suggesting weekly homework
might do wonders for some

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:55.640
but be completely off-putting
for others.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:59.560
So why can therapy be better
than talking to a friend?

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.680
For starters, you get
your therapist's undivided attention

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:04.920
and you don't have to worry
about what they think of you.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:06.360
You can cry your eyes out.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:07.960
They won't judge you.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:09.880
Unlike with a friend,
you don't have to worry

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:11.760
about hurting
your therapist's feelings.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:13.840
Then there's confidentiality.

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:15.040
Unless the therapist thinks

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:16.960
you're at risk of harming
yourself or others,

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.960
whatever you say
in the therapist's office stays

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.000
in the therapist's office.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:24.760
A friend might offer advice, which,
even if well intended,

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:26.640
might not be right for you.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:29.920
A good therapist usually won't offer
advice, but instead listen,

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:33.000
summarise what you said
and reflect it back to you.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:35.080
This simple technique
can help you see things

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:36.840
from a different perspective.

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.480
Unlike a friend who may be quick
to agree with you,

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:42.520
a therapist can challenge you
in a supportive way,

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:43.960
and without being challenged,

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:47.640
you may not find new ways
of thinking or behaving.

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:51.520
Of course, therapy doesn't work
for everyone in every situation.

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:54.520
Serious mental health conditions
can require drug treatment

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.960
or even an inpatient stay.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:58.880
Other people find
self-help techniques

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:00.920
such as meditation
are enough for them.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:05.160
But overall, studies show
that a typical client is better off

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:07.360
for having gone through the process.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:09.200
In fact, according to the NHS,

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:13.040
in 2022, over 66% of people

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:15.520
who entered therapy
found it beneficial.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.600
In the words of American writer
Carlee J Hansen,

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:22.520
"Therapy didn't fix me,

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:25.280
"therapy gave me the tools
to help me fix myself,

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:28.760
"over and over again.
For the rest of my life."

